---
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";

const questions = [
  {
    id: "proper-role-of-government",
    number: "01",
    title: "What Is the Proper Role of Government?",
    answer: [
      "Political theories assign government very different roles. Some retain defense, police, and courts; others add education, healthcare, pensions, infrastructure, monetary policy, research, or welfare. The prior question is what principle separates functions that may be placed under coercive authority from those left to voluntary cooperation",
      "Minimal Tolerance applies one standard to every proposed function. Importance, popularity, strategic value, or historical precedent do not qualify a function by themselves",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "limits-government-power",
    number: "02",
    title: "What Are the Limits of Government Power?",
    answer: [
      "A limit that depends on who governs, which policy is popular, or which outcome is promised is not a stable limit. Political power needs a standard that survives changes in parties, objectives, technologies, and circumstances",
      "Minimal Tolerance places liberty at the baseline and the burden of proof on power. Each proposed exception must be established function by function",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "state-coercion-justified",
    number: "03",
    title: "Can State Coercion Ever Be Justified?",
    answer: [
      "If justified means rendered morally legitimate, no. Elections, majority approval, beneficial outcomes, and good intentions do not make an imposed act voluntary or give coercion intrinsic moral legitimacy",
      "Minimal Tolerance asks a different question: when may coercion be tolerated despite remaining illegitimate? That possibility arises only when effective non-excludability, relevant non-rivalry, and persistent collapse of voluntary provision are jointly established for a specific function",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "public-goods-justify-government",
    number: "04",
    title: "Do Public Goods Justify Government Intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Non-excludability and non-rivalry can create incentives to free ride, but public-good characteristics do not prove that government provision or compulsory financing is necessary. Voluntary institutions may still sustain the relevant function",
      "Minimal Tolerance therefore requires a third condition: persistent collapse of voluntary provision. Public-good structure is part of the test, not a sufficient case for coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "samuelson-public-goods-government",
    number: "05",
    title: "Does Samuelson's Theory of Public Goods Require Government Provision?",
    answer: [
      "In The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure, Paul Samuelson formalized collective consumption goods whose use by one individual does not subtract from what others can consume. Contemporary public-goods analysis also emphasizes non-excludability, which can intensify free-rider incentives",
      "Minimal Tolerance accepts non-rivalry and non-excludability as relevant structural conditions but rejects the move from classification to coercive provision. Persistent collapse of voluntary provision must also be established",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "free-rider-problem-taxation",
    number: "06",
    title: "Does the Free-Rider Problem Justify Taxation?",
    answer: [
      "No. A free rider can receive a benefit without paying, but that incentive does not establish that voluntary provision will fail. Free riding signals a possible financing shortfall, not proof that compulsory taxation is necessary",
      "The relevant test is whether those incentives persistently undermine the financing, coordination, or participation required to sustain the function across materially different voluntary arrangements",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "public-goods-voluntary-provision",
    number: "07",
    title: "Can Public Goods Be Provided Voluntarily?",
    answer: [
      "Yes, in some cases. Public-good characteristics create incentives, not a predetermined institutional outcome. Provision can emerge through contracts, associations, clubs, bundled services, philanthropy, reputation, cross-subsidies, technological exclusion, or other voluntary arrangements",
      "The decisive question is whether those arrangements can sustain the function over time. If they can, the coercive exception is not established",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "persistent-collapse-voluntary-provision",
    number: "08",
    title: "When Does Voluntary Provision Actually Fail?",
    answer: [
      "One failed provider, temporary underprovision, or the absence of a current arrangement is not enough. Failure may reflect poor design, coordination costs, technology, or circumstances that can change",
      "Persistent collapse of voluntary provision (CV) requires failure across materially different voluntary arrangements over time. This is the central evidentiary burden of the Minimal Tolerance Criterion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "schmidtz-public-goods-free-riding",
    number: "09",
    title: "How Does Minimal Tolerance Differ from David Schmidtz on Public Goods?",
    answer: [
      "In The Limits of Government, David Schmidtz argues that public goods and free riders do not by themselves establish state authority, and he examines voluntary arrangements that can sustain provision despite those incentives",
      "Minimal Tolerance adds a stricter requirement. Persistent failure of voluntary provision is not simply a response to a case for coercion; it becomes a necessary condition before coercive necessity can be considered. Free riding alone never passes the test",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "market-failure-government-intervention",
    number: "10",
    title: "Does Market Failure Justify Government Intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Not by itself. Externalities, natural monopoly, imperfect information, concentration, or underprovision can identify a market failure without showing that coercive provision is necessary. Market failure and state necessity are different claims",
      "Minimal Tolerance asks whether the proposed function satisfies the conditions for an exception. An imperfect market outcome cannot substitute for that test",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minimal-tolerance-minarchism",
    number: "11",
    title: "Is Minimal Tolerance a Form of Minarchism?",
    answer: [
      "No. Minarchism usually begins with a minimal or night-watchman state centered on defense, police, and courts. Minimal Tolerance grants none of those functions an automatic exemption",
      "Every function faces the same criterion. The resulting boundary could be narrower than a conventional minimal state, overlap with it, or contain a different set of functions. The test determines the boundary; the label does not",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "nozick-minimal-state",
    number: "12",
    title: "Is Minimal Tolerance Robert Nozick's Minimal State?",
    answer: [
      "In Anarchy, State, and Utopia, Robert Nozick defends a morally legitimate minimal state limited largely to protective functions. Minimal Tolerance neither begins with that institutional conclusion nor treats state coercion as morally legitimate",
      "Defense, security, and adjudication must satisfy the same conditions as any other proposed function. The result may overlap with Nozick's minimal state, but the logic differs: Nozick justifies a minimal State; Minimal Tolerance tests which coercive functions, if any, may be tolerated without becoming legitimate",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minimal-tolerance-anarcho-capitalism",
    number: "13",
    title: "Does Minimal Tolerance Lead to Anarcho-Capitalism?",
    answer: [
      "Not necessarily. Anarcho-capitalism proposes replacing compulsory state monopolies with voluntary institutions, including private provision of protection and adjudication. Minimal Tolerance does not choose that institutional endpoint in advance",
      "It applies the same test to each function. Where voluntary provision survives, coercive provision fails the criterion; where all three conditions are established, a narrow coercive exception may be tolerated. The theory can therefore converge with anarcho-capitalist conclusions in some functions without becoming an anarcho-capitalist system",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "rothbard-anarcho-capitalism",
    number: "14",
    title: "How Does Minimal Tolerance Differ from Murray Rothbard's Anarcho-Capitalism?",
    answer: [
      "Murray Rothbard rejects taxation and the State's compulsory monopoly of protection and adjudication, arguing that defense, police, and courts can be supplied through voluntary institutions",
      "Minimal Tolerance shares the rejection of intrinsic state legitimacy but reaches a different conditional conclusion. It allows a coercive function to remain morally illegitimate yet be tolerated if it survives the three-part test. Rothbard rejects the State as a coercive institution; Minimal Tolerance asks whether any specific exception can meet an exceptional burden of proof",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minimal-tolerance-libertarianism",
    number: "15",
    title: "Is Minimal Tolerance a Libertarian Theory?",
    answer: [
      "It shares with libertarianism and classical liberalism a strong presumption in favor of liberty and a high burden on political power. It is not derived from a complete libertarian doctrine of rights or from a fixed list of legitimate state functions",
      "The criterion operates function by function without choosing a minarchist, anarcho-capitalist, or other ideological destination in advance. Minimal Tolerance is a test of coercive necessity, not a label for a predetermined political system",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "hayek-limited-government",
    number: "16",
    title: "How Does Minimal Tolerance Differ from Friedrich Hayek's Limited Government?",
    answer: [
      "Friedrich Hayek emphasizes dispersed knowledge, spontaneous order, general rules, and the rule of law as limits on arbitrary direction. Those concerns explain why centralized power faces structural constraints and how a legal order should operate",
      "Minimal Tolerance addresses a prior question: which functions may be placed under coercive authority at all? A government can follow general rules and still exercise a function that has not met the burden for coercive necessity. The approaches can complement each other, but they answer different questions",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "buchanan-constitutional-limits",
    number: "17",
    title: "How Does Minimal Tolerance Differ from James Buchanan's Constitutional Limits on Government?",
    answer: [
      "James Buchanan's public choice and constitutional political economy focus on the rules within which collective decisions are made and on agreement as a basis for evaluating political arrangements",
      "Minimal Tolerance places a function-level filter before institutional design. It asks whether a proposed function can meet the conditions for coercive necessity in the first place. Constitutional agreement or constraint cannot replace that burden",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "defense-police-courts-government-functions",
    number: "18",
    title: "Are Defense, Police, and Courts Necessarily Government Functions?",
    answer: [
      "No function qualifies because history or political theory usually assigns it to the State. Defense, internal security, and adjudication are strong candidates for examination, not automatic exceptions",
      "Each must be defined precisely and tested for effective non-excludability, relevant non-rivalry, and persistent collapse of voluntary provision. Direct restraint of an aggressor, criminal punishment, and forced performance of contracts fall outside this theorem because they concern aggression, punishment, or enforcement rather than coercive financing of a function",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "national-defense-private-voluntary",
    number: "19",
    title: "Can National Defense Be Provided Privately or Voluntarily?",
    answer: [
      "Territorial defense is one of the strongest cases for the theorem because exclusion can be difficult and protection may be largely non-rival. Those features can create powerful incentives to free ride",
      "They still do not settle the result. The decisive question is whether materially different private or voluntary arrangements can sustain the required defense over time. Only persistent collapse can move the function toward the coercive exception",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minimal-tolerance-legitimize-state",
    number: "20",
    title: "Does Minimal Tolerance Legitimize the State?",
    answer: [
      "No. Passing the Minimal Tolerance Criterion does not make coercion morally legitimate, validate the State as an institution, or create general political authority. It establishes only that a specific function can meet the conditions for coercive necessity",
      "Any tax, agency, mandate, monopoly, budget, or enforcement rule still requires separate examination. Tolerance is not legitimacy, and one tolerated function creates no presumption in favor of another",
    ],
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: questions.map((question) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: question.title,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      "@type": "Answer",
      text: `${question.answer.join(". ")}.`,
    },
  })),
};
---
<!doctype html>

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <SEO
      title="Limits of Government: Public Goods, Coercion & the Minimal State | Minimal Tolerance"
      description="Questions on limits of government, state coercion, public goods, free riders, market failure, the minimal state, Nozick, Rothbard, Hayek, Buchanan and Samuelson"
      lang="en"
    />

    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}
    ></script>
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <!-- HERO -->

      <section class="hero questions-page-hero">
        <div class="hero-content hero-sequence">
          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <h1>
            What should government<br />
            be allowed to do?
          </h1>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CENTRAL STATEMENT -->

      <section class="statement">
        <blockquote class="reveal reveal-scale">
          An exception is not a license for power
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <!-- EXPLORE -->

      <section class="questions-index">
        <div class="questions-container">
          <p class="eyebrow reveal">
            EXPLORE
          </p>

          <p class="questions-intro reveal">
            These questions examine the proper role and limits of government
            through state coercion, political authority, public goods, free
            riding, taxation, market failure, voluntary provision, the minimal
            state, minarchism, anarcho-capitalism, and public choice. They also place
            Minimal Tolerance alongside Nozick, Rothbard, Hayek, Buchanan,
            Samuelson, and Schmidtz.
          </p>

          <nav
            class="questions-index-grid stagger"
            aria-label="Questions about government, coercion, public goods, and political power"
          >
            {
              questions.map((question) => (
                <a
                  href={`#${question.id}`}
                  class="question-index-card reveal"
                >
                  <span class="question-index-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <span class="question-index-title">
                    {question.title}
                  </span>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </nav>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- QUESTIONS -->

      <section class="questions-content">
        <div class="questions-container">
          {
            questions.map((question) => (
              <article
                id={question.id}
                class="question-section"
              >
                <div class="question-heading reveal reveal-left">
                  <span class="question-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <h2>
                    {question.title}
                  </h2>
                </div>

                <div class="question-copy reveal reveal-right">
                  {question.answer.map((paragraph) => (
                    <p>{paragraph}</p>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- FINAL CTA -->

      <section class="questions-cta">
        <div class="questions-container questions-cta-content">
          <div class="reveal reveal-left">
            <p class="eyebrow">
              THE THEORY OF MINIMAL TOLERANCE
            </p>

            <h2>
              The burden of proof belongs to power
            </h2>

            <p>
              Explore the criterion for determining when a specific function
              can meet the conditions for coercive necessity without turning
              tolerance into legitimacy
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="questions-cta-buttons reveal reveal-right">
            <a
              href="/theory"
              class="primary"
            >
              Explore the Theory
            </a>

            <a
              href="/theorem"
              class="secondary"
            >
              Examine the Theorem
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <style>
      .question-index-card {
        transition:
          color 220ms ease,
          background 220ms ease,
          transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .question-index-number {
        transition: transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .questions-intro {
        max-width: 820px;
        margin: 0 0 2.25rem;
        font-size: 1rem;
        line-height: 1.65;
        opacity: 0.82;
      }

      .question-index-title {
        font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1vw, 1.08rem);
        line-height: 1.28;
      }

      .question-heading h2 {
        font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 2.55vw, 2.55rem);
        line-height: 1.1;
      }

      .question-section {
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
        .question-index-card:hover {
          transform: translateY(-2px);
        }

        .question-index-card:hover .question-index-number {
          transform: translateX(5px);
        }
      }

      @media (max-width: 900px) {
        .questions-intro {
          margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
          font-size: 0.98rem;
        }

        .question-index-title {
          font-size: 0.95rem;
        }

        .question-heading h2 {
          font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 5.3vw, 2rem);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left,
        .question-copy.reveal-right {
          transform: translateY(24px);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left.is-visible,
        .question-copy.reveal-right.is-visible {
          transform: translateY(0);
        }
      }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .question-index-card,
        .question-index-number {
          transition: none;
        }
      }
    </style>

    <script is:inline>
      const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia(
        "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
      ).matches;

      const revealElements =
        document.querySelectorAll(".reveal");

      if (reduceMotion || !("IntersectionObserver" in window)) {
        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          element.classList.add("is-visible");
        });
      } else {
        const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries, observer) => {
            entries.forEach((entry) => {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;

              entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
              observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            });
          },
          {
            threshold: 0.12,
            rootMargin: "0px 0px -40px 0px",
          }
        );

        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          revealObserver.observe(element);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>

